    "collection": "Victoria & Albert Museum, London",
    "movement": "Italian Radical Design",
    "wikiTitle": "Joe Colombo (designer)",
    "description": "A modular storage trolley constructed from ABS plastic injection-molded components that stack and rotate on a central spine. Each drawer can be removed and reconfigured, with the entire unit rolling on four casters. The system demonstrates how industrial materials and manufacturing processes can create flexible domestic storage that adapts to user needs rather than dictating fixed arrangements.",
    "significance": "It demonstrates how modular design thinking translates abstract systems theory into practical objects. Shows how Italian designers used industrial plastics not just for cost reduction but to enable new kinds of user interaction and spatial flexibility.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 11,
        "type": "sameProblem",
        "reason": "Colombo’s Boby rolls to where you need it — storage that follows you. The 606 stays where you mount it — storage that defines the room. Mobile Italian plastic vs. fixed German aluminium. The 1970s argued about whether furniture should move or stand still."
      },
      {
        "id": 12,
        "type": "zeitgeist",
        "reason": "Colombo’s Boby is a storage system that actually works beautifully — rotating drawers, stacking modules, smooth ABS plastic. Carlton is a storage system that deliberately doesn’t work well — angled shelves, impractical heights. 1970s Italian industrial logic meeting 1980s Italian anti-logic."
      },
      {
        "id": 2,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Colombo systematises ABS plastic moulding into a modular storage tower — Italian rationalism in polymer. The Castiglionis bolt a tractor seat to a steel stem — Italian wit in found objects. Two Milanese approaches to industrial material: engineer it into a system, or grab it off a shelf."
      },
      {
        "id": 10,
        "type": "material",
        "reason": "Colombo's Boby trolley exploits ABS plastic's ability to create complex rotating compartments in a single moulding — plastic enabling spatial ingenuity. The Grillo exploits ABS plastic's ability to create two shells connected by a precision hinge — plastic enabling mechanical ingenuity. Both are 1960s Italian demonstrations that injection-moulded plastic enables new functional behaviours."
      }
    ],
