    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "500 metres of red cotton rope hand-wound around an aluminium and steel frame in a seemingly random pattern that actually follows a precise winding logic. The rope creates both the seat surface and the visual identity — no upholstery, no covering, just rope and structure. Each chair takes hours of hand-winding and no two are identical despite following the same method.",
    "significance": "The piece that established the Campanas internationally. It demonstrates that 'handmade' doesn't have to mean 'primitive' — the winding technique requires genuine skill and the result is both structurally sound and visually extraordinary. The red rope is simultaneously structure, surface, and decoration.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 2,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "The Campanas' Vermelha (1998): 500 metres of rope hand-wound around a steel frame — the material's behaviour is the form. The Castiglionis' Mezzadro (1957): a tractor seat's existing shape mounted on a stem — the found object's form is the design. Two ways of letting material do the designer's work."
      },
      {
        "id": 55,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "The Campanas wind 500 metres of rope around a steel frame — the material's tangled behaviour IS the upholstery, every chair unique. Vignelli routes millions of passengers through a colour-coded grid — the system's logical behaviour IS the navigation, every journey legible. Order and chaos: two systems that work because they commit fully."
      }
    ],
